1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
PDPA and Archiving in Singapore: How Long Can You Keep Personal Data?. A guide for Singapore records and compliance teams

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,642
The PDPA does not set a fixed retention period.

3
00:00:11,642 --> 00:00:23,076
Under section 25, an organisation may keep personal data only while the purpose it was collected for is still served, or while it is needed for legal or business purposes.

4
00:00:23,076 --> 00:00:31,500
After that, the data must be destroyed or anonymised. Moving records into an archive does not count as ceasing to retain them.

5
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:39,480
No fixed period: retention is judged by reasonableness. No "just in case" retention without a legal or business reason.

6
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:43,704
Tax, company, employment and sector rules can still require set periods.

7
00:00:43,704 --> 00:00:53,500
The PDPC recommends a retention policy with different periods for different data and a stated reason for longer retention. Source: PDPC Advisory Guidelines, Chapter 18

8
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:02,486
A locked cabinet, a warehouse or restricted access does not count as ceasing to retain. Archived personal data stays under the PDPA.

9
00:01:02,486 --> 00:01:11,500
The Protection Obligation (section 24) also applies: the archive needs reasonable security arrangements. Source: PDPC Advisory Guidelines, Chapter 18

10
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:19,181
1. Destroy: so records cannot be retrieved, for example shredding paper or securely deleting files. 2.

11
00:01:19,181 --> 00:01:27,500
Return or transfer: to the individual, or as the individual instructs. 3. Anonymise: so the data can no longer identify anyone.

12
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:37,237
1. Classify records: by type and the personal data they hold. 2. Set a retention period: for each class, with the reason recorded. 3.

13
00:01:37,237 --> 00:01:46,500
Restrict access: and log who retrieves what. 4. Schedule disposal: and record what was destroyed and when. 5. Review: the schedule regularly.

14
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:54,443
Scanning does not change the rules: a scanned image is still personal data and inherits the retention period.

15
00:01:54,443 --> 00:02:03,869
Microfilm suits records with a long legal basis, such as permanent registers and statutory records. Individual frames cannot be deleted from a roll.

16
00:02:03,869 --> 00:02:07,500
Put records with the same disposal date on the same roll.

17
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:17,933
How long can I keep personal data? As long as the purpose is served or a legal or business reason exists. Is a scanned copy covered? Yes.

18
00:02:17,933 --> 00:02:29,500
A scanned image containing personal data is personal data. What counts as destroying data? Making it irretrievable, or anonymising it so it no longer identifies anyone.

